<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salem, Massachusetts</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salem is a coastal city in Essex County, Massachusetts, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. Settled in 1626 and taken over in 1628 by an advance party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massachusetts-bay-company">Massachusetts Bay Company</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-endecott">John Endecott</a>, it was one of the first towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. It became notorious for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-witch-trials">Salem witch trials</a> of 1692, and in the late 18th and early 19th centuries grew rich from overseas trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salem-massachusetts.jpg" alt="Salem, Massachusetts" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fletcher6 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federal_Street_District.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
